.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt NFANON 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nfanon
.Nd anonymize the IP addresses 
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm 
.Fl r Ar path
.Op Fl w Ar nffile
.Op Fl C Ar config
.Fl K Ar key
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl v Ar level
.Op Fl V
.Op Fl W Ar num
.Op Fl x Ar key=value
.Op Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
anonymizes all IP addresses ( src, dst, next hop, router IP, NAT, tunnel
endpoints, and the recording exporter's own IP, etc. ) in the netflow
records using the CryptoPAn (Cryptography-based  Prefix-preserving Anonymization)
module. The key -K is used to initialize the Rijndael cipher. The key is either
a 32 character string, or a 64 hex digit string starting with 0x. See
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crypto-PAn for more information on CryptoPAn.
.Pp
Anonymization is prefix-preserving: two original addresses sharing an
.Ar N Ns -bit
prefix are guaranteed to share the same
.Ar N Ns -bit
prefix after anonymization, for the same
.Fl K
key. This holds across all files anonymized with the same key, which
allows aggregate and subnet-level analysis to continue working on
anonymized data without revealing the original addresses.
.Pp
The source specified by argument
.Fl r Ar path
may point to a single nfdump file or to a directory containing many nfdump files.
All files in a directory are processed recursively.
.Pp
If the output argument
.Fl w Ar nffile
is given, all anonymized records are written into that single file, even if the 
source is a directory. If no argument
.Fl w
is specified, 
.Nm
overwrites the original source file with the anonymized flow records.
If the source is a directory, each flow file is anonymized respectively.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl r Ar path
Path to read flow files to anonymize. Path may point to a single file or a directory
containing many flow files. Required.
.It Fl w Ar nffile
File name to write anonymized flow records to. If this argument is missing, the source
file name is taken, which means the original file is overwritten.
.It Fl K Ar key
.Ar key
is either a 32 character string, or a 64 char hex string starting with 0x. This key
is used to initialize the anonymizer. Required.
.It Fl C Ar config
Read configuration from
.Ar config .
Use
.Fl C Ar none
to disable configuration-file loading for this invocation.
.It Fl s
Preserve source addresses. All other supported addresses are anonymized.
.Fl s
and
.Fl d
may not both be given, since that would leave nothing to anonymize.
.It Fl d
Preserve destination addresses. All other supported addresses are anonymized.
See
.Fl s
above.
.It Fl v Ar level
Set verbose level. A verbose level of 0 disables progress output.
.It Fl W Ar num
Set the core limit to
.Ar num
CPU cores.
Reader decompression threads, anonymization worker threads, and writer compression
threads are derived automatically from this budget.
Setting
.Ar num
to 0 (the default) uses all available online CPU cores.
If
.Ar num
exceeds the number of online CPU cores, a warning is logged and the budget
is clamped to the available core count.
.Pp
Individual role counts are configured with the
.Em [nfanon.threads]
or
.Em [common.threads]
sections in
.Xr nfdump.conf 5 ,
or overridden per-run with
.Fl x Ar threads.workers=N
(anonymization threads),
.Fl x Ar threads.readers=N
or
.Fl x Ar threads.writers=N
(0 restores the auto-derived count).
.It Fl V
Print version and exit.
.It Fl x Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
Override a single configuration key for this run only.
May be repeated to override multiple keys.
See
.Xr nfdump.conf 5
for the list of available keys.
.It Fl h
Print help text to stdout and exit.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
To create a random 64 character hex string you may use the following command:
.Dl % xxd -u -l 32 -p -c 64 /dev/urandom
Use the resulting output as key, prepended with 0x as -K argument.
or
.Dl % openssl rand -hex 16
and use this output as argument for -K
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Nm
returns 0 on success and a nonzero value on error (1 or 255, depending on
the failure).
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nfdump 1
